Z||000000||btoth||02-17-2001||06:48 PM||||i was just curious if anyone has had the barrings (sp?) replaced in their engine before and about how much it cost to have it done.  thanks for any info.||||reg||1
Z||000001||gabriel||02-17-2001||08:02 PM||||Barrings?<P>  I'm not quite sure what you mean by 'barrings', but if you mean 'bearings', you are looking at quite a job.  1st, you have to remove the engine, separate it from the trans, bring the block to a machine shop to have the engine taken apart, (unless you are going to do that yourself) then they  measure & true the journals, & then have the correct oversize (actually undersize) bearings installed.<BR>   It is possible to have the bearings installed in the engine without pulling it, but I wouldn't advise that - except in an emergency.<BR>  <BR>  You didn't state which engine you are working on, but any engine/machine shop can quote you prices.  I don't advise going to a dealer to have the work done, but don't let me stop you.<P>  Plus, if you are replacing bearings, you had better consider reconditioning the rings, bores, & valves.<BR> <BR>   With most Fiero engines,  I believe that it might be WAY, way cheaper to replace/exchange the engine, rather than having the machine work done piecemeal.<P>  Good luck!<P>   <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||SteveJ||02-17-2001||09:43 PM||||Tell us what makes you think it NEEDS bearings.||||reg||1
Z||000003||btoth||02-18-2001||04:19 PM||||woops, sorry for the lack of info.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  the oil pressure drops off a lot at idle in my 2.8 and when i had the engine rebuilt a year ago the guy mentioned the "bearings" were probably worn and that was what was causing the lack of presure at idle.  i don't know much about engines obviously.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>he had said that he could do it for about $500 (he's a friend of my uncle's) but i know it would take him months to do it.  so i was just wondering what people had paid for similar work.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||02-18-2001||04:57 PM||||If the engine is not knocking from too much rod or main bearing clearance, it is probably the cam bearings causing the low oil pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000005||gabriel||02-18-2001||05:02 PM||||<BR>Somethings not right here.<P>Any engine rebuild includes new bearings, that's fundamental.<P>  Did your friend's uncle do the rebuild?<P>  You could be right, it might well be the bearings, if a machinest was stone dumb enough not to include them in a rebuild, but check other stuff first, like the oil pump.<P>  Or perhaps you are confused about what was rebuilt?  Maybe just a valve job perhaps?<P>  That would explain it.<P>  Anyway, check around the machine shops & go to some performance shops to see who they recommend.  $500 doesn't sound  bad, especially if he's pulling the engine & re-installing it.<P>  Let us know & good luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||btoth||02-18-2001||05:59 PM||||Yeah, it was my uncle's friend that did the rebuild.  Had to replace the head gaskets so that's as low as he went: machined the heads and got new valves, rods, seals.  The engine seems to run just fine and doesn't make any noises out of the ordinary.  Maybe I'll just let it go and see if anything gets worse over time.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks for the responses though!||||reg||1
